  "success": true,
  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon (Next.js 16 / Drizzle / Postgres)\n\nThis is a small, unusually disciplined codebase — almost everything is a Server Component reading Postgres directly, TanStack Query is deliberately scoped to one polling surface, and the data layer already batches what would normally be N+1s (`Promise.all`, grouped `countChildrenByParent` lookups, etc.). So most of the \"classic\" anti-patterns aren't present. Below are the concrete, real findings per category — I skipped categories where I couldn't find an actual problem rather than inventing one.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Missing indexes — the real \"N+1\" risk here\n\nThere are **no loop-per-row queries**, but there's also only **one index in the entire schema** (`entities_run_source_external_idx`). Every other foreign key — `conflicts.runId`, `conflicts.matchId`, `matches.runId`, `resolutions.conflictId`, `auditLog.runId`, `runs.sourceAId/sourceBId/rulesetId` — is unindexed. Postgres does **not** auto-index FK columns (unlike MySQL), so nearly every query in `lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `audit.ts`, `export.ts`, and `runs.ts` currently does a sequential scan filtered down from the whole table.\n\nThis is worse than a classic N+1 at scale: `getQueuePage` (opening the conflict queue) filters `conflicts` by `runId` with no index — once you have a few runs' worth of history, every queue open, every count, every audit page scans the full table.\n\n`lib/db/schema.ts`:\n```ts\nexport const runs = pgTable(\"runs\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"runs_started_at_idx\").on(table.startedAt),        // listRuns / getLatestRunWithConflicts ORDER BY\n]);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId),\n]);\n\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_id_severity_idx\").on(table.runId, table.severity), // getQueuePage/getQueueCounts\n  index(\"conflicts_match_id_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"resolutions_conflict_id_idx\").on(table.conflictId), // every LEFT JOIN in conflicts.ts/export.ts\n]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"audit_log_run_id_created_at_idx\").on(table.runId, table.createdAt), // listRunAudit/getAuditPage\n]);\n\n// entities: add a second index — countChildrenByParent groups by (sourceId, parentExternalId),\n// which the existing (runId, sourceId, externalId) index doesn't cover.\nexport const entities = pgTable(\"entities\", {\n  // ...\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"entities_run_source_external_idx\").on(table.runId, table.sourceId, table.externalId),\n  index(\"entities_run_parent_idx\").on(table.runId, table.parentExternalId),\n]);\n```\nRun `npm run db:generate && npm run db:migrate` after. This is the single highest-leverage change in the codebase.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary React re-renders — `ConflictFocus` list\n\n`components/conflicts/conflict-focus.tsx:57-66` maps up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE` (100, `types/queue.ts:134`) rows into `<ListRow>` on every render. `ListRow` isn't memoized, so any state change in the parent — moving the keyboard selection (`selectedIndex`), a decision landing (`decided`), or `pendingId` toggling — re-renders **all 100 rows**, even though only one or two actually changed:\n\n```tsx\n{rows.map((candidate, index) => (\n  <ListRow\n    key={candidate.conflictId}\n    row={candidate}\n    index={index}\n    selected={index === selectedIndex}   // changes for 2 rows out of 100\n    status={decided[candidate.conflictId]?.status ?? candidate.resolution?.status ?? null}\n    onSelect={() => onSelect(index)}     // new closure every render\n  />\n))}\n```\n\nFix — memoize the row and stabilize the callback so React can bail out for the 98 unaffected rows:\n```tsx\nconst ListRow = memo(function ListRow({ row, index, selected, status, onSelect }: Props) {\n  // ...unchanged body\n});\n\n// in the parent, key the callback by id instead of a fresh closure per item:\nconst handleSelect = useCallback((index: number) => onSelect(index), [onSelect]);\n```\n`FocusPane`'s `submit={submit(row.conflictId)}` (line 75) is fine — it only affects the single focused row, not the list.\n\n*(Note: `ConflictFocus`/`use-review` aren't wired to a route yet — no `app/(console)/conflicts/page.tsx` exists, only the API route. Worth fixing before it ships, since 100-row re-render churn is exactly the kind of thing that's invisible in dev with 5 test rows and visible in review with a real queue.)*\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n**a. `listSources()` / `listRulesets()` / `getActiveRuleset()` hit Postgres on every navigation.** They call `connection()` (`lib/server/sources.ts:18`, `rulesets.ts:20,32`), which forces fully dynamic rendering on every request — appropriate for `runs`/`conflicts`, wasteful for sources/rulesets, which change rarely (a config screen, a versioned policy). Since these are read from multiple pages (`sources/page.tsx`, `rules/page.tsx`, `runs/new/page.tsx`), cache them and invalidate only on write:\n\n```ts\n// lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { unstable_cache, revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport const listSources = unstable_cache(\n  async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n    const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n    return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n  },\n  [\"sources-list\"],\n  { tags: [\"sources\"] },\n);\n\nexport async function createSource(input: CreateSource): Promise<Source> {\n  const [row] = await getDb().insert(sources).values(input).returning();\n  revalidateTag(\"sources\");\n  return sourceSchema.parse(row);\n}\n```\nSame pattern for `rulesets.ts` (tag `\"rulesets\"`, revalidate in `createRulesetVersion`).\n\n**b. `GET /api/runs/[runId]/status` reads the DB unconditionally on every 2-second poll**, even though the row is only needed as a terminal-state check / fallback (`app/api/runs/[runId]/status/route.ts:23-36`). For an in-flight run with an open tab, that's ~30 DB round trips/minute doing nothing but confirming \"still running.\" Low severity for a single-tenant internal tool, but worth knowing it's there if multiple reviewers leave run pages open simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks\n\nNone found. Specifically checked and confirmed clean:\n- `Elapsed` (`components/runs/run-live.tsx:98-118`) — `setInterval` is cleared in the `useEffect` cleanup.\n- `RunProgress`'s `useQuery` polling — TanStack Query owns its own interval/subscription lifecycle and unsubscribes on unmount.\n- `ListRow`'s `scrollIntoView` effect — no subscription, nothing to clean up.\n- The Postgres client (`lib/db/index.ts`) is intentionally cached on `globalThis` to survive Next.js HMR reloads in dev — correct pattern, not a leak.\n\nOne thing to *monitor*, not fix: `postgres(url, { prepare: false })` has no explicit `max` connections. Fine for `next start` on a single long-running server; if this ever moves to a serverless/edge deployment, each cold-started instance opens its own pool against Supabase's pooler, so an explicit `max: 5`-ish cap would be worth adding at that point.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx` recomputes evaluation order and dirty-diffing on **every keystroke in any rule field**, without memoization:\n\n```tsx\nconst nextVersion = (active?.version ?? 0) + 1;\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);          // line 61 — new array + sort, every render\nconst dirty = !active || name !== active.name || !sameRules(rules, active.rules); // line 65 — O(n) every render\n```\nAnd the render loop makes it O(n²) on top of that:\n```tsx\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);          // O(n) lookup...\n  return (\n    <RuleRow key={rule.id} rule={rule} position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1} ... /> // ...twice, per row\n  );\n})}\n```\nGiven a ruleset is realistically a few dozen rows, this is cheap in absolute terms — but it's a one-line fix and the pattern (recompute + `indexOf` in a map) is worth not repeating elsewhere as the app grows:\n```tsx\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst dirty = useMemo(\n  () => !active || name !== active.name || !sameRules(rules, active.rules),\n  [active, name, rules],\n);\n// and precompute positions once instead of calling indexOf per row:\nconst positions = useMemo(() => new Map(ordered.map((r, i) => [r.id, i + 1])), [ordered]);\nconst indices = useMemo(() => new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i])), [rules]);\n```\n\n---\n\n### Summary, in priority order\n\n| # | Finding | Impact | Effort |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Missing indexes on `conflicts.runId`, `resolutions.conflictId`, `auditLog.runId`, etc. | High — every conflict/audit read scans full tables as data grows | Low (schema + migration) |\n| 2 | `ConflictFocus` list rows unmemoized | Medium — 100-row re-render on every keystroke/selection once wired up | Low |\n| 3 | Sources/rulesets re-fetched every navigation | Low-medium — needless DB round trips on a low-write config screen | Low |\n| 4 | No memory leaks found | — | — |\n| 5 | `RulesetEditor` recomputes order/dirty every render | Low — small N, but O(n²) pattern worth not repeating | Low |\n\nThe codebase's existing discipline (batched queries, server-first rendering, scoped client state) means the highest-value fix by far is #1 — it's the one place where \"small dataset today, full table scan tomorrow\" is baked into the schema rather than the code.\n",
